\f1\fs22 \'93You could drive a truck through here,\'94 Johnny said as he looked about the enormous curving corridor. \'93Two trucks each way.\'94\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
<!$Scr_Ps::0><$Scr_Ps::1>
\f1\fs22 \'93Have you never been down here before?\'94 Sarah sat next to Johnny in the service vehicle\'97Johnny called them golf carts.\
\'93I just design things,\'94 Johnny answered. \'93Not build them. Eloise\'92s engineers are adequate for clicking the bits together. They work fast, I can give them that.\'94\
Chip grumbled. He had somehow wedged his hulking frame in the back seat. His hair touched the roof. Somehow, he managed to swivel his frame enough to look back at the service vehicle following behind. In it, the Sages sat like priests at a ceremony: Kleo and Solo in front, Chilo in back. Chip should\'92ve taken the chance to ride with the Sages; who knows what they are cooking up back there.\
\'93I should brought a packed lunch. This corridor goes on forever,\'94 Johnny said.\
\'93Would you rather spin Gyges discs in your office ensuite?\'94 Chip grumbled.\
Johnny looked about as he thought about that. He clapped his hands twice to test the echo, but the whirring of the vehicle\'92s electric motor swallowed the sound. The corridor still felt cold and empty. \
\'93Ensuite, huh?\'94 Johnny said. \'93Perhaps you could use a Gyges disc for more efficient flushing. Or cleaning.\'94\
Chip laughed. He pictured a grotesquely liquified Johnny being flushed down a toilet by a Gyges disc.\
The vehicle passed though a pair of heavy doors that presumably could be shut remotely. They were the third pair they\'92d passed. Johnny had counted. He looked into the corners where the black roof met the roughly rendered concrete walls. He was sure there was surveillance equipment up there somewhere, even though he couldn\'92t see any. Occasionally, they passed closed side-doors you could drive a truck through too.\
Eventually, there was proverbial light at the end of the tunnel. The last side door was open. The two vehicles drove through.\
The glare blinded Sarah momentarily. Once her eyes adjusted, everything still appeared sharper than she knew they could be. Kleo came to the vehicle and assisted her out. She took a moment to get balanced on her pregnancy-laden feet.\
It was almost silent. Air conditioning quietly hummed, possibly from a postcode away.\
Sarah tried to look up to the roof of the cavern, but felt unsteady on her heels, as if Little Clinton\'92s weight might topple her backwards. He gave her a sharp kick, as if to say, \'93Damn straight.\'94\
Dr Quark detached himself from a glitch of lab coats, approached her, and extended his hand. She grasped and shook it with ascetic restraint while she let her left hand on her belly.\
\'93Welcome to the to the laundry,\'94 Dr Quark said. \'93We\'92re rather proud of this one.\'94\
She was only dimly aware of Johnny and Chip behind her right shoulder. She had no awareness of the where the Sages were.\
\'93It\'92s not quite how I envisioned it,\'94 Johnny said, nearing Sarah\'92s flank, \'93but Quark is staking his cousin\'92s gonads on it being serviceable.\'94\
To Sarah, the new 
\f2\i washing machine
\f1\i0 , as the engineers and technicians liked to call it, appeared more like a small radio telescope, maybe three stories tall, with a huge netball hoop right where the dish should be. Attached to the hoop were two identical contraptions that looked like they may have been assembled from surplus Murderbot  parts. Those were presumably where the Gyges discs were buried.\
\'93It\'92s very\'85 yellow,\'94 Sarah observed.\
\'93The colour is called Nuclear Custard,\'94 Johnny corrected. \'93The paint store let me name it.\'94\
Sarah nodded. Though there were components as black as carbon, and others as silver and polished as one of Eloise\'92s saucepans, the overall effect was of a giant canary with a lighting rig bolted to its head.\
\'93Perhaps we should inspect it from the bleachers,\'94 Dr Quark said. He waved his arm back and presented his hand, ringleader style, in the direction of a stand of benches beside a parked truck.\
Sarah smiled. \'93Thank you.\'94 She followed Dr Quark.\
\'93Tax!\'94 Immediately behind Sarah, Johnny cursed. He\'92d tripped to his knees over a circular railway on which the washing machine could clearly travel.\
\'93Did you forget that you designed that part?\'94 Sarah asked.\
Johnny wiped his mouth while he tested whether he could put weight on his ankle.\
Dr Quark led them across the floor, around wheeled maintenance stands; alignment tripods; mobile power/control cabinets; cable drums; counterweight blocks; a portable gantry or small lifting rig; tool cabinets. One one of the stands rested a spare disc harness assembly. The Sages passed by the clutter, touching things like they were bored, until they found something interesting enough to circle around.\
Dr Quark, Johnny, Chip and Sarah arrived at a stand of metal tubing and plastic benches. He offered Sarah his hand to assist her up the stairs, but she waved it away. At the top of the stairs, Sarah stood at the railing and looked back. She felt like a spectator at a high school ball game.\
\'93The view is better up top,\'94 Dr Quark said.\
\'93I think I can see enough here, thank you.\'94\
Dr Quark nodded deferentially and came to Sarah\'92s side; Chip flanked her on the other. Sarah thought she could hear Chip grinding his teeth. Johnny was making a racket as he ran up the tiers as if he was showing off to his mother how high he could go.\
Sarah took in the sight that was the new washing machine. \'93It looks elaborate.\'94\
\'93I assure you it is designed only to facilitate the kinds of motion and orientation that the data from previous machines told us we needed. With this apparatus we will be able to fine-tune macro movement while reducing the need to spin the discs so fast.\'94\
\'93Please tell me you didn\'92t let Johnny anywhere near it,\'94 Sarah said.\
Chip scoffed.\
\'93The days of duct tape and vacuum cleaner parts are over, Ms Jacobson,\'94 Dr Quark said.\
Chip groaned. \'93You make that sound like it\'92s a good thing.\'94\
There was an awkward silence.\
\'93Oh, it\'92s an ellipse!\'94 Sarah grinned. \'93From down there, I thought it was a circle.\'94\
Dr Quark nodded. \'93Proportions and perspective do tend to get distorted looking up at something like this.\'94\
\'93The whole thing is distorted from wherever you look,\'94 Chip said.\
\'93Um\'85\'94 Dr Quark pulled a pen from his pocket, realised there was nothing he wanted to do with it, and put it back. \'93The whole design shift is underpinned by our insight that geometry is more efficacious  than brute rotational speed.\'94\
Sarah had been keeping one eye out for the Sages moving about the washing machine. Chilo had  been standing well back, viewing it through a rectangle made with his thumbs and forefingers. He kept moving a few metres sideways and repeating the view finding. Sometimes he crouched. He couldn\'92t have looked sketchier if he was a photographer at a porn shoot.\
Dr Quark mistook Sarah\'92s silence as leaving room for a thorough explanation. \'93The problem with spinning discs in close proximity is they immediately start affecting each other, and pushing each other out of phase.\'94\
Chip sounded like he\'92d cracked a tooth right out of its socket. He stared straight ahead.\
\'93Johnny\'92s premise was that spinning disks in proximity prevented them discharging in our dimensional space,\'94 Sarah said.\
\'93That\'92s only the first hurdle. The next one is phase stability.\'94\
Chip\'92s growling under his breath confirmed for Sarah that they were all beginning to sing from the same multidimensional hymn book. If Chip restricted his parts to basso grumble, Sarah could live with that.\
\'93To stabilise and focus, they need the discs to be constantly adjusting their geometric relationship  with respect to one another,\'94 Sarah prompted.\
\'93That is a reasonable description of their behaviour in our space.\'94 Dr Quark was almost as pleased as Chip wasn\'92t. \'93In our space the relationship among the discs maintains what we call rubber topological integrity.\'94\
\'93Like you can draw a triangle on a sheet of rubber,\'94 Sarah said, \'93then stretch the rubber and deform the triangle\'97it\'92s no longer a Euclidean triangle because its angles are all wrong and the sides are bent.\'94\
\'93Quite so.\'94\
\'93But because it\'92s only the rubber that\'92s changed, and the triangle is merely mapped over the distorted rubber plane, it can be said the distorted shape is still a triangle with respect to the distorted rubber co-ordinates.\'94\
\'93Correct,\'94 Dr Quark said.\
\'93What\'92s all this about distorted rubber coordinates, Sarah?\'94 Johnny had just returned to the group from his wandering among the nosebleed seats. Are looking for something too big for your condoms? I might be able to help you out there.\'94\
\'93Good,\'94 Sarah answered. \'93Because I want to pull a condom over your head and suffocate you with,\'94 Sarah said.\
\'93I love it when you flirt with me.\'94\
The trans-postcode air-conditioning suddenly sounded silent, as if it had been infested with crickets.\
Chip leaned in and whispered, \'93You know, I could disjoint every bone in that little man\'92s body.\'94\
\'93It\'92s okay. I\'92ve had plenty of those offers,\'94 Sarah said as she watched Kleo fuss around the base of the washing machine. She had to check what she thought she\'92d just seen. Kleo appeared to spit on the cuff if his long medieval sleeve then start to polish one of the machine\'92s Nuclear Custard components.\
Sarah reclaimed her train of thought. \'93I suppose Euclidean space could be said to be just a special case of rubber space.\'94\
Dr Quark laughed. \'93Ha, ha! Yes! Quite so!\'94\
\'93The I suppose the radio-telescope-style machinery orients order the tilt of the elliptical hoop\'97\'94\
\'93It\'92s actually a maglev rail, Sarah,\'94 Johnny scolded. \'93You can\'92t reach the orbit speeds with anything mechanical.\'94\
Dr Quark rolled his eyes. \'93He\'92s right there. The maglev is a critical feature of the design. And you are right about the design drawing from radio telescopes. There\'92s already the circular track, as you\'92ve identified. Then the main cradle pivots through elevation on the side bearings. Those geared sectors carry the tilt load, although most of the actual weight is balanced through the counterweights behind the frame. It\'92s essentially the same trick used by very large radio telescopes: if you balance the mass properly, you don\'92t need heroic motors to move it, although you might need heroic motors to stop it doing anything you didn\'92t ask for.\'94 Dr Quark chortled.\
\'93Then, there\'92s two arms, one for each disc,\'94 Sarah continued, \'93that allow the discs to be placed above or below the plane of the ecliptic\'85\'94\
\'93As well as vary their distance from the centre of the elliptic,\'94 Dr Quark added. \'93Go on.\'94\
\'93The arms run around teh maglev rail, and presumably can be completely opposite each other or right next to each other on the rail.\'94\
\'93Correct.\'94\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \'93Then there are secondary articulated arms that\'85 allow finer control of the discs with respect to the rail plane? \'85 no, that just seems more of the same.\'94 Sarah thought. \'93Do the secondary arms rotate?\'94\
Dr Quark clapped his hands and pointed at Sarah. \'93Yes!\'94\
\'93So while the main arm is travelling around the ellipse, the secondary arm can carry the disc in a smaller circle around the end of the main arm.\'94\
\'93Independently,\'94 Dr Quark said.\
Sarah looked more closely at the machinery hanging from the ends of the secondary arms. \'93And that thing is a refined version of the old disc harness.\'94\
\'93Yes.\'94\
\'93Which still spins the disc end-over-end.\'94\
Johnny and Dr Quark nodded together.\
\'93Independently of everything else?\'94 Sarah asked.\
Dr Quark smiled. \'93That\'92s rather the point. But that crowning achievement is that the controlling software monitors all the telemetry and actively match every aspect of motion to harmonise and stabilise the discs.\'94\
\'93Oh, my apocalyptic underwear!\'94 Chip blurted.\
\'93Even the spin rate of the discs?\'94 Sarah asked.\
Dr Quark nodded. \
Sarah\'92s lips thinned for a moment, then she moved on. \'93How long before it\'92s fully operational?\'94\
\'93Tomorrow,\'94 Johnny answered and straightened his suit coat..\
Dr Quark grimaced, paralysed his eyes open and looked nothing in particular. \'93The washing machine is  assembled. It it yet to run. We need to check the maglev carriage under load, run the arms through their full travel, balance and calibrate the elevation system, verify the azimuth drive, test interlocks/emergency stops, 
\f2\i then
\f1\i0  do staged live commissioning.\'94\
\'93Right,\'94 Sarah summarised. \'93Well, it seems you too have a plan to put together. Carry on. Johnny, I\'92ll send the golf cart back for you.\'94\
Johnny stammered. \'93You\'92re leaving me here?\'94\
Sarah made for the stairs to the floor.\
Johnny blocked Sarah\'92s path and hissed into her ear as he stared at Chip. \'93Don\'92t let that Fridge Fraud delay my schedule.\'94\
Sarah stared Johnny out of her way. As she descended the stairs she watched Solo climb into the parked truck, slide the driver\'92s seat back and forward, adjust both mirrors, and test the steering wheel. He might even have peered over his shoulder as though deciding whether the turning circle suits him.\
